<#
.SYNOPSIS
Configures a geography-based Local-In Policy to restrict IPsec VPN access (IKE/ESP) to specific countries on a FortiGate interface.

.DESCRIPTION
This script performs a multi-step security configuration on a FortiGate unit. First, it defines two geography-based firewall addresses for Qatar (QA) and Sri Lanka (LK). These are then aggregated into a firewall address group named "Allowed-Geography". Finally, the script modifies the Local-In Policy table—which controls traffic destined for the FortiGate unit itself—to allow IKE and ESP traffic from these specific regions while explicitly denying all other IKE/ESP traffic on the 'port1' interface. This is a common hardening technique used to reduce the attack surface of VPN gateways against global brute-force or DDoS attempts.

.EXPECTED RESULT
FortiOS Configuration Change: The creation of two geography address objects, one address group, and two local-in-policy entries (ID 1 and ID 99).

.EXAMPLE
The following script would implement a similar logic but for a different service like HTTPS management, restricting administrative access to a specific geographic region.
#>


config firewall address

edit "Qatar"
      set type geography
      set country "QA"
    next
    edit "SL"
      set type geography
      set country "LK"
    next
end

config firewall addrgrp

edit "Allowed-Geography"
    set member "Qatar" "SL"
next
end

config firewall local-in-policy
   edit 1
      set intf "port1"
      set srcaddr "Allowed-Geography"
      set dstaddr "all"
      set action accept
      set service "IKE" "ESP"
      set schedule "always"
   next
end
config firewall local-in-policy
   edit 99
      set intf "port1"
      set srcaddr "all"
      set dstaddr "all"
      set action deny
      set service "IKE" "ESP"
      set schedule "always"
    next
end